# The two workload shapes that are bounded by WORK rather than by time — and the reason the plan
# panel's seconds field goes away for both of them (`workloadSeconds` returns `null`): an iteration
# count states how much work there is, never how long it will take. How long it takes is the
# measurement.
#
# **A count is the right shape whenever the work does not wait on anything.** A rate against a body
# that does no I/O runs until it saturates the generator, and a saturated run marks every threshold
# `skipped` and reports numbers about tflw rather than about the target. Both tests below do real
# HTTP, so either shape would work — they are here because the pane must be able to read and write
# both, and because `run … per user across …` is the one shape whose sentence names two numbers
# that mean different things.

@catalogue @workload
test "forty catalogue reads, shared out across four readers"
  run 40 iterations across 4 users
  api GET /products
  expect status equals 200
  threshold p95 duration is less than 2s
  threshold error rate is less than 5%

@catalogue @workload
test "ten category reads each, across four readers"
  run 10 iterations per user across 4 users
  api GET /categories
  expect status equals 200
  threshold error rate is less than 5%
